<!-- ─── §01 THE PROBLEM ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>What RAE promised — and where it fell short</h2>

<p>Traditional image diffusion models (Stable Diffusion, FLUX, etc.) work in the latent space of a VAE: a small convolutional encoder compresses images to 4–16× smaller feature maps, the diffusion transformer adds noise and denoises there, then a decoder reconstructs the image. It works, but the VAE latent space was designed for reconstruction, not for a diffusion model to reason in.</p>

<p><strong>Representation Autoencoders (RAE)</strong> swap the VAE for a pretrained vision encoder (DINOv2, DINOv3, CLIP…). The encoder's features are richer — they capture semantic structure that helps diffusion converge faster. But the original RAE recipe had three blind spots:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Only the final encoder layer was used.</strong> A 24-layer DINOv3 was reduced to its last layer alone — discarding 23 layers of complementary spatial and structural information.</li>
  <li><strong>RAE couldn't use classifier-free guidance (CFG).</strong> CFG requires an unconditional forward pass for comparison; RAE's representation-conditioned formulation breaks this. A separate smaller model (AutoGuidance) had to be trained and run at every inference step — doubling complexity and compute.</li>
  <li><strong>RAE was assumed to replace REPA.</strong> REPA (Representation Alignment) distills the same pretrained features to intermediate diffusion layers. Since RAE already uses those features as the latent space, everyone assumed REPA would be redundant. Turns out this assumption was wrong.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="callout-icon">↯</span>
  <div class="callout-body">RAEv2 fixes all three with no new architecture components: sum the last K encoder layers, add REPA back on top, and reformulate the output head as x-prediction for free internal guidance. Together they deliver <strong>over 10× faster convergence</strong> to the same quality level.</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §02 K-LAYER ENCODER ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-klayers" data-code="klayers">
<div class="section-no">02 — K-Layer Encoder</div>
<h2>Summing K encoder layers: a free reconstruction-generation knob</h2>

<p>The insight is simple: a pretrained vision encoder isn't just its final layer. Layer 1 sees edges and textures; layers 12–16 hold spatial structure; the final layer encodes semantic concepts. The original RAE threw away everything except the last layer.</p>

<p>The fix: define the encoder representation as the <em>sum</em> of the last K layers. No new parameters, no training cost — just change which features go into the latent space.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Generalized encoder output (sum of last K layers)</div>
  <span class="math-var">x</span> = <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>L−K+1</sub> + <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>L−K+2</sub> + … + <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>L</sub>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// K=1 recovers original RAE</span>
</div>

<p>The key insight is that higher K trades generation quality for reconstruction quality. The original RAE (K=1) has rFID 0.60 — noticeably blurry reconstructions. At K=7, guided gFID hits its best value of 1.06 while rFID drops to 0.35. At K=23 (all layers), rFID reaches 0.18 — competitive with proprietary VAEs — while guided gFID stays near 1.10.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
