<p>The key insight is that everything is computed in a <em>single forward pass</em>. The REPA head branches off early layers and produces a weak x-prediction. The main output head uses all layers for a strong prediction. Both outputs live in the same representation space — so they can be combined for guidance arithmetically, with no second model needed.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §04 COMPLEMENTARY MECHANISMS ────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-complementary" data-code="complementary">
<div class="section-no">04 — Complementary Roles</div>
<h2>RAE and REPA do different things — use both</h2>

<p>The prevailing assumption was: since RAE already uses the pretrained encoder features as the latent space, distilling those same features to intermediate diffusion layers (REPA) would be a wasteful skip connection. Why would adding REPA on top of RAE help?</p>

<p>The authors tested this assumption empirically across <strong>27 vision encoders</strong> and found the opposite. REPA on top of RAE consistently improved generation quality across every single encoder tested. The two methods work through <em>different mechanisms</em>:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>RAE</strong> benefits from encoders with high <em>global semantic accuracy</em> (ImageNet linear probe = LP). The diffusion model learns to reason in a semantically-rich latent space.</li>
  <li><strong>REPA</strong> benefits from encoders with high <em>spatial structure quality</em> (local distance similarity score = LDS). It regularizes how intermediate diffusion features relate to each other spatially.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">encoder property → generation quality · Pearson r with gFID (lower is better)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click mode to switch</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="comp-tabs">
      <button class="comp-tab active" data-mode="rae">RAE alone</button>
      <button class="comp-tab" data-mode="repa">REPA alone</button>
      <button class="comp-tab" data-mode="both">RAE + REPA</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="comp-canvas"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The scatter plots tell the story clearly: for RAE alone, LP (global semantics) has strong negative correlation with gFID (r = −0.81) while LDS (spatial structure) barely correlates (r = −0.13). For REPA alone, it's inverted: LDS dominates (r = −0.89). When combined, the best predictor is the <em>average</em> of LP and LDS (r = −0.83) — confirming they contribute complementary information.</p>

<p>This complementarity also explains why DINOv3-L is the best encoder for RAEv2: it scores highly on both LP and LDS, while DINOv2-B (the original RAE's default encoder) is strong on LP but weaker on LDS.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §05 FREE GUIDANCE ────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-guidance" data-code="guidance">
<div class="section-no">05 — Free Guidance</div>
<h2>REPA head = free weaker model for internal guidance</h2>

<p>CFG needs two things: a strong (conditioned) prediction and a weak (unconditioned) baseline. The guidance signal is their difference — amplified by weight w. The problem: RAE's latent space is conditioned on the representation, making unconditional generation ill-defined. So CFG doesn't work with RAE. The original workaround was AutoGuidance: train a second, smaller diffusion model to serve as the weak baseline.</p>

<p>RAEv2 eliminates this entirely. The key observation: the REPA head is already the weaker prediction. It only accesses early intermediate layers and uses a lightweight MLP — by definition, it's less capable than the full model. Reformulate both the full model output and the REPA head as x-prediction (rather than velocity), and you can combine them directly:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Internal guidance (no extra model or forward pass)</div>
  x&#770;<sub>guided</sub> = x&#770;<sub>full</sub> + <span class="math-var">w</span> · (x&#770;<sub>full</sub> − x&#770;<sub>repa</sub>)
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// x&#770;_full: all DiT layers · x&#770;_repa: REPA head on early layers only</span>
</div>

<div class="guidance-grid">
  <div class="guidance-card bad">
    <div class="guidance-label bad">Broken with RAE</div>
    <div class="guidance-name">Classifier-Free Guidance</div>
    <div class="guidance-desc">Requires an unconditional pass — undefined in RAE's representation-conditioned latent space. CFG actually degrades gFID with RAE.</div>
    <div class="guidance-cost bad">2× forward passes</div>
    <div class="fwd-row">
      <div class="fwd-box main">cond. pass</div>
      <div class="fwd-plus">+</div>
      <div class="fwd-box uncond">uncond. pass ✗</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="guidance-card ok">
    <div class="guidance-label ok">Original RAE workaround</div>
    <div class="guidance-name">AutoGuidance</div>
    <div class="guidance-desc">Train a second, smaller DiT model. Run it at every inference step as the weak baseline. Extra training, extra memory, extra compute.</div>
    <div class="guidance-cost ok">1× pass + separate model</div>
    <div class="fwd-row">
      <div class="fwd-box main">main DiT</div>
      <div class="fwd-plus">+</div>
      <div class="fwd-box weak">small DiT ★</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="guidance-card best">
    <div class="guidance-label best">RAEv2 — free</div>
    <div class="guidance-name">REPA Internal Guidance</div>
    <div class="guidance-desc">The REPA head (already trained for alignment) is the weak prediction. Combine with main output. No extra model. No extra forward pass.</div>
    <div class="guidance-cost best">1× forward pass, no extra model</div>
    <div class="fwd-row">
      <div class="fwd-box main">main output</div>
      <div class="fwd-plus">⊕</div>
      <div class="fwd-box repa">REPA head</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The ablation confirms: CFG fails (gFID 3.86 vs 3.75 without guidance), AutoGuidance helps (3.31), and REPA internal guidance achieves the best result (1.06 at 80 epochs) at zero extra cost.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §06 RESULTS ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
